---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { BudgetChart } from '../../components/islands/BudgetChart'
import { budget, debt, vintageOf } from '../../data'

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="whole-budget">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 4</span></p>
    <h2>One axis, everything on it</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Above the line, outlays stacked by category with revenue drawn across. Below it, the
      deficit that gap produces. Colour marks who held all three levers.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      FY2025: $7.01 trillion out, $5.24 trillion in, $1.78 trillion short.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Federal outlays from fiscal 1962 to 2025 stacked into mandatory, discretionary and net interest as a share of GDP, with a revenue line across them and the deficit drawn below zero. A three-row strip along the top shows which party held the presidency, House and Senate each year. Shaded bands mark the 2008 to 2009 financial crisis and the 2020 to 2021 pandemic."
      title="Federal outlays, revenue and the deficit, fiscal 1962 to 2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="outlays, revenue and deficit in nominal $ trillions, real FY2025 $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="Mandatory outlays are charted net of offsetting receipts, so the three stacked segments sum to total outlays. Party control is curated for FY1995 onward only; earlier years show no control strip."
    >
      <BudgetChart client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Outlays and the deficit are the same arithmetic seen twice. Splitting them across two
      panels lets each be read on its own scale and hides how small the deficit is next to the
      budget that produces it. Here the deficit bar is the distance between the revenue line and
      the top of the stack.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.slice(1).filter((s) => s.id !== 'whole-budget').map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
